  "slug": "knowledge-graph-887e",
  "summary": "Ligand pose, scoring, and antibody predictors are being re-evaluated under leakage control and structural stratification.",
  "synthesis": "The shared thread is scrutiny of how structure-informed predictors are evaluated. A scoring-function case study attributes inflated ML benchmark performance to pocket bias and structural leakage; after removal, ML models decline while AutoDock Vina generalizes. An AlphaFold3 antibody benchmark over 3401 complexes against 23798 negatives reports about 53% maximum recall and 3% false positives, with biases by target size and structure method. Mavchen 1 reports conformational ensembles beating static AlphaFold2 structures on 21 of 29 targets. Ptarmigan-1 takes a structure-free route, co-embedding residues and molecules from sequence and 2D chemistry. The set is heterogeneous; what is new is pairing stratified, leakage-aware evaluation with explicit structural or conformational inputs rather than any single model class.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-07-31"
